WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.533 --> 00:00:02.758
- [Voiceover] Two tornadoes
ongoing right now.

00:00:02.758 --> 00:00:04.872
- [Voiceover] Oh my God.

00:00:04.872 --> 00:00:08.004
(upbeat band music)

00:00:08.004 --> 00:00:11.591
- [Voiceover] It takes a certain craziness

00:00:11.591 --> 00:00:14.524
for people to go out
and pursue these things.

00:00:14.524 --> 00:00:16.083
- [Voiceover] God, oh my God.

00:00:16.083 --> 00:00:17.831
- The raw destruction of nature

00:00:17.831 --> 00:00:20.283
in such a tight, enclosed area.

00:00:20.283 --> 00:00:23.041
It's tragic, it's amazing, it's beautiful

00:00:23.041 --> 00:00:24.874
and every one, for me,

00:00:26.202 --> 00:00:28.539
holds that sense of awe and wonder.

00:00:28.539 --> 00:00:29.671
Here it comes.

00:00:29.671 --> 00:00:32.110
My name is Tony Laubach
and I am a storm chaser

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:33.390
and meteorologist.

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:35.395
I basically make a living out in the field

00:00:35.395 --> 00:00:38.179
selling imagery to news
outlets, telling the story

00:00:38.179 --> 00:00:43.126
in a visual sense, of what's
happening with weather.

00:00:43.126 --> 00:00:45.070
From March to June,
I'm basically traveling

00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:47.794
all over the country in
search of severe weather,

00:00:47.794 --> 00:00:49.666
literally could be
putting tens of thousands

00:00:49.666 --> 00:00:50.693
of miles on my car.

00:00:50.693 --> 00:00:52.978
Sometimes 5000 miles a week.

00:00:52.978 --> 00:00:56.313
Large tornado on the
ground, west of Dodge City.

00:00:56.313 --> 00:00:59.937
The storm we've been on is
weakening pretty heavily

00:00:59.937 --> 00:01:00.792
right now.

00:01:00.792 --> 00:01:01.962
The storm coming out of the South

00:01:01.962 --> 00:01:03.740
I think, has got my attention right now.

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:05.225
It's something I'm kinda watching.

00:01:05.225 --> 00:01:06.760
(banjo music)

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:09.488
Kansas has always been my holy land.

00:01:09.488 --> 00:01:13.345
The flatlands, the fields, the colors.

00:01:13.345 --> 00:01:15.859
For a normal person, you
drive from Kansas City

00:01:15.859 --> 00:01:18.945
to Colorado and you
gotta spend eight hours

00:01:18.945 --> 00:01:20.285
driving through nothing.

00:01:20.285 --> 00:01:22.721
To chasers, to people like me,

00:01:22.721 --> 00:01:24.637
it's the most beautiful
landscape of anything.

00:01:24.637 --> 00:01:28.532
You can see tens if not
hundreds of miles at a time

00:01:28.532 --> 00:01:31.666
and watch these storm
from a great distance.

00:01:31.666 --> 00:01:33.651
It's something truly amazing.

00:01:33.651 --> 00:01:34.854
There it is.

00:01:34.854 --> 00:01:38.133
We as chasers never, ever want storms

00:01:38.133 --> 00:01:40.088
to impact anybody in a negative way.

00:01:40.088 --> 00:01:43.481
And unfortunately, in
some instances, they do,

00:01:43.481 --> 00:01:44.520
significantly.

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:49.041
They take lives and peoples
homes and their livelihoods.

00:01:49.041 --> 00:01:50.185
(piano music)

00:01:50.185 --> 00:01:53.352
The lows are devastating but the highs

00:01:55.030 --> 00:01:57.607
are amazing, because of the gratification,

00:01:57.607 --> 00:02:00.081
the beauty, everything that you have put

00:02:00.081 --> 00:02:01.878
the effort into is there.

00:02:01.878 --> 00:02:05.540
When you get rewarded by
nature for your efforts,

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:08.933
and you see this event in
person, there's nothing

00:02:08.933 --> 00:02:10.766
that compares to that.

